# Next.js 16 — what changed

This repo runs Next.js 16.2.10. Version-matched documentation ships inside the package
at `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`, mirroring the structure of nextjs.org/docs. **Read
the relevant page there before writing framework code.** The upgrade notes below are
condensed from `01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-16.md`; consult it for anything not
covered.

Requirements: Node.js 20.9+, TypeScript 5.1+. The App Router runs React 19.2.

## Traps that bite hardest

**Turbopack is the default** for both `next dev` and `next build`. The `--turbopack`
flag is obsolete — this repo's scripts are correctly plain `next dev` / `next build`.
Don't add the flag back. A custom `webpack` config now *fails* the build; opt out with
`--webpack` if you ever truly need it.

**`next lint` was removed and `next build` no longer runs ESLint.** Lint is a separate
step (`npm run lint` → bare `eslint`). The `eslint` option in the Next config is also
gone. TypeScript still runs during the build.

**ESLint flat config is the default** for `@next/eslint-plugin-next`, which is what
`eslint.config.mjs` already uses. Don't migrate back to `.eslintrc`.

**Async request APIs are async-only.** `cookies()`, `headers()`, `draftMode()`, and
`params` / `searchParams` in `page`, `layout`, `route`, and `default` must be awaited.
The synchronous compatibility shim from v15 is gone. `params` and `id` in
`opengraph-image`, `twitter-image`, `icon`, `apple-icon`, and `sitemap` are now Promises
too. (Not currently relevant here — this app has no dynamic routes — but it's the most
common way older code breaks.)

**`middleware` is renamed to `proxy`.** The file is `proxy.ts` and the named export is
`proxy`. The proxy runtime is `nodejs` and cannot be configured; the `edge` runtime is
not supported there. Config flags renamed accordingly (`skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize` →
`skipProxyUrlNormalize`).

**`next dev` writes to `.next/dev`,** separate from `next build`'s `.next`, so the two
can run concurrently. A lockfile prevents two `next dev` instances on the same project.

**Next.js no longer overrides `scroll-behavior` during navigation.** Opt back in with
`data-scroll-behavior="smooth"` on `<html>`. This repo sets
`html { scroll-behavior: smooth }` in CSS and does *not* set the attribute — correct
here, because the site is a single route with only anchor navigation.

## Caching and PPR

The experimental PPR flag and the `experimental_ppr` route segment config are removed.
PPR is now opted into with the top-level `cacheComponents: true`, and it behaves
differently from the v15 canaries — see
`01-app/02-guides/migrating-to-cache-components.md`. `experimental.dynamicIO` and
`experimental.useCache` are deprecated in favor of the same flag.

`cacheLife` and `cacheTag` are stable — import them from `next/cache` without the
`unstable_` prefix. `revalidateTag` now requires a second `cacheLife` argument
(`revalidateTag('posts', 'max')`); the one-argument form is a TypeScript error. New:
`updateTag` for read-your-writes in Server Actions, and `refresh` to refresh the client
router.

None of this is used in this repo yet — it's fully static — but reach for the right API
if you add caching.

## `next/image` defaults changed

`minimumCacheTTL` is 4 hours (was 60s), `qualities` defaults to `[75]` only, `16` was
dropped from `imageSizes`, redirects are capped at 3, local IP optimization is blocked,
and local sources with query strings need `images.localPatterns.search`. `images.domains`
is deprecated in favor of `remotePatterns`, and `next/legacy/image` is deprecated.

## Removed outright

AMP (`next/amp`, `useAmp`, the `amp` config), `serverRuntimeConfig` /
`publicRuntimeConfig` (use env vars), `unstable_rootParams`, and the `appIsrStatus` /
`buildActivity` / `buildActivityPosition` dev indicator options.

## Also worth knowing

- Turbopack config moved out of `experimental` to a top-level `turbopack` key.
- The React Compiler option (`reactCompiler: true`) is stable but off by default.
- Parallel route slots now require an explicit `default.js` or the build fails.
- `next build` output no longer reports `size` / `First Load JS`; use Lighthouse or
  Vercel Analytics to measure real route performance instead.
